{"kind":"report","text":"Slice 9 ruling: native declaration admission (answers request 63cf995e).\n\n1. Linearization. Native declaration admission takes place at the fresh synchronous consumption fence, before the payload, with no await between the check and the use. Preparation only stages and charges bytes. Preparation is not an admission point.\n\n2. Lifetime. The native-code configuration is fixed for each pinned platform version. Platform data and the reviewed policy are trusted, reviewed code, and they must not change while a process runs. A change is a defect in trusted code. The generated static artifact is a build product of the reviewed source, and the generator check in the gate detects drift between the two.\n\n3. Transient changes. The product contract does not require the room to refuse a change to trusted configuration that is reverted before consumption. At the fence, the room admits the reviewed artifact A only if the live platform data and the reviewed policy are equal to A. A change that remains at the fence refuses. A wrapper replacement refuses. In your counterexample the room admits A. A is the reviewed configuration in force where the act takes effect, so this result is correct.\n\n4. Recorded difference. The static route does not observe a change that is reverted during preparation. The original route observed it. State this as a named, accepted difference in the slice 9 implementation note. Keep three witnesses: a change and revert during preparation admits A; a change that remains at consumption refuses; a wrapper replacement refuses. Nonalias sources keep the original route.\n\n5. Next step. Continue the static route under the original scalar prices and caps, with the work limit of 67,108,864. An immutable native-data construction, a freeze, a proxy or a cache is not necessary for this. The v16 Directory genesis failure stays recorded as a natural failure. Claim a fit only from a run at the reviewed head.","body":{"to":"builder"}}